<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Epic · c. 1800 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Epic of Gilgamesh</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="akk">Shūtur eli sharrī (&quot;Surpassing All Other Kings&quot;)</bdi> / <bdi lang="akk-Latn">Sha naqba īmuru (&quot;He Who Saw the Deep&quot;)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The oldest great story we know: a proud king of Uruk finds a true friend, loses him, and goes to the ends of the earth to learn why we must die.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (the Standard Babylonian version was compiled by Sîn-lēqi-unninni)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (no music for it survives)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Babylonian and Assyrian Mesopotamia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Mesopotamia (present-day Iraq)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>Old Babylonian version 18th century BCE; Standard Babylonian version between the 13th and 10th centuries BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Epic</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (an ancient text; the transliteration and translation printed here were published by Jastrow and Clay in 1920)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>, from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, is the world's oldest surviving example of narrative literature. Its history begins with five Sumerian poems about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, some of which may date to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> (c. 2100 BCE). These independent stories were later used as material for a combined epic in Akkadian. The first surviving version of it, the &quot;Old Babylonian&quot; version, dates to the 18th century BCE and is named after its opening words, Shūtur eli sharrī, &quot;Surpassing All Other Kings&quot;; only a few of its tablets survive. The later Standard Babylonian version, compiled by Sîn-lēqi-unninni between the 13th and 10th centuries BCE, opens Sha naqba īmuru, &quot;He Who Saw the Deep&quot;. About two thirds of its twelve tablets have been recovered, some of the best copies from the library of the 7th-century BCE Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p><p>In the first half of the story <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, two-thirds god and one-third man, oppresses his people, and the gods create the wild man Enkidu as his equal. Tamed by Shamhat, Enkidu comes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and fights Gilgamesh; the two become friends, travel to the Cedar Forest, kill its guardian Humbaba and, when the goddess Ishtar sends the Bull of Heaven against them, kill the Bull too. The gods condemn Enkidu to die of an illness. In the second half, Gilgamesh, stricken by his friend's death and afraid of his own, journeys to the end of the world to find <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Utnapishtim</a>, the only man who survived the great flood with his wife and was given eternal life. He learns that immortality is not for him; a plant that restores youth is stolen by a serpent, and he returns to Uruk, whose great walls he praises.</p><p>About 15,000 fragments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets were found in the library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austen-henry-layard">Austen Henry Layard</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuzd-rassam">Hormuzd Rassam</a> and W. K. Loftus in the early 1850s. In 1872 George Smith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> read translated fragments, among them the flood story, before the Society of Biblical Archaeology, and he published fuller translations in 1875 and 1876. The words printed here are from the second tablet of the Old Babylonian version, in the edition of Morris Jastrow and Albert T. Clay (1920): <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> tells his mother two dreams, and she explains that a companion is coming to him. That edition read the hero's name as &quot;Gish&quot;; Erech is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>. Andrew George's critical edition of 2003 is now the standard one.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The epic asks what a life is worth if it must end. Its hero begins as a tyrant, is changed by friendship, is broken by grief, and searches for a way to escape death, only to learn that the gods kept life for themselves and gave death to humankind. What remains to him are the things people make and share: a friend, a well-ruled city, the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and the story itself.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Akkadian (transliteration)</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Akkadian (transliteration)</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">it-bi-e-ma Giš šú-na-tam i-pa-áš-šar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Gish sought to interpret the dream;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">iz-za-kàr-am a-na um-mi-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spoke to his mother:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">um-mi i-na šá-at mu-ši-ti-ia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;My mother, during my night</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">šá-am-ḫa-ku-ma at-ta-na-al-la-ak</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I became strong and moved about</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">i-na bi-ri-it it-lu-tim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>among the heroes;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ib-ba-šú-nim-ma ka-ka-bu šá-ma-i</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And from the starry heaven</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[ki]-iṣ-rù šá A-nim im-ḳu-ut a-na ṣi-ri-ia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A meteor(?) of Anu fell upon me:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">áš-ši-šú-ma ik-ta-bi-it e-li-ia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bore it and it grew heavy upon me,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ú-ni-iš-šú-ma nu-uš-šá-šú ú-ul il-ti-’i</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I became weak and its weight I could not endure.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">Uruk ma-tum pa-ḫi-ir e-li-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The land of Erech gathered about it.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">it-lu-tum ú-na-šá-ku ši-pi-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The heroes kissed its feet.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ú-um-mi-id-ma pu-ti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It was raised up before me.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">i-mi-du ia-ti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They stood me up.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">áš-ši-a-šú-ma ab-ba-la-áš-šú a-na ṣi-ri-ki</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bore it and carried it to thee.”</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">um-mi Giš mu-di-a-at ka-la-ma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The mother of Gish, who knows all things,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">iz-za-kàr-am a-na Giš</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spoke to Gish:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">mi-in-di Giš šá ki-ma ka-ti</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>“Some one, O Gish, who like thee</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">i-na ṣi-ri i-wa-li-id-ma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the field was born and</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ú-ra-ab-bi-šú šá-du-ú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Whom the mountain has reared,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ta-mar-šú-ma [kima Sal(?)] ta-ḫa-du at-ta</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou wilt see (him) and [like a woman(?)] thou wilt rejoice.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 42 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">it-lu-tum ú-na-šá-ku ši-pi-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heroes will kiss his feet.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">tí-iṭ-ṭi-ra-áš-[šú tu-ut]-tu-ú-ma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou wilt spare [him and wilt endeavor]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ta-tar-ra-[as-su] a-na ṣi-[ri]-ia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To lead him to me.”</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[uš]-ti-nim-ma i-ta-mar šá-ni-tam</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He slept and saw another</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[šú-na]-ta i-ta-wa-a-am a-na um-mi-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Dream, which he reported to his mother:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[um-mi] a-ta-mar šá-ni-tam</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[“My mother,] I have seen another</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[šú-na-tu a-ta]-mar e-mi-a i-na su-ḳi-im</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[Dream.] My likeness I have seen in the streets</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[šá Uruk] ri-bi-tim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[Of Erech] of the plazas.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ḫa-aṣ-ṣi-nu na-di-i-ma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>An axe was brandished, and</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">e-li-šú pa-aḫ-ru</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They gathered about him;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">ḫa-aṣ-ṣi-nu-um-ma šá-ni bu-nu-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And the axe made him angry.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">a-mur-šú-ma aḫ-ta-du a-na-ku</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I saw him and I rejoiced,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">a-ra-am-šú-ma ki-ma áš-šá-tim</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I loved him as a woman,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">a-ḫa-ab-bu-ub el-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I embraced him.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">el-ki-šú-ma áš-ta-ka-an-šú</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I took him and regarded him</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">a-na a-ḫi-ia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As my brother.”</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">um-mi Giš mu-da-at [ka]-la-ma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The mother of Gish, who knows all things,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[iz-za-kàr-am a-na Giš]</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[Spoke to Gish]:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[Giš šá ta-mu-ru amêlu]</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[“O Gish, the man whom thou sawest,]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">[ta-ḫa-ab-bu-ub ki-ma áš-šá-tim el-šú]</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[Whom thou didst embrace like a woman].</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">áš-šum uš-[ta]-ma-ḫa-ru it-ti-ka</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>(means) that he is to be associated with thee.”</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="akk-Latn">Giš šú-na-tam i-pa-šar</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Gish understood the dream.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Morris Jastrow and Albert T. Clay, An Old Babylonian Version of the Gilgamesh Epic (Yale University Press, 1920): the Pennsylvania Tablet, col. I-II. Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/An_Old_Babylonian_Version_of_the_Gilgamesh_Epic%2FThe_Pennsylvania_Tablet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">An Old Babylonian Version of the Gilgamesh Epic/The Pennsylvania Tablet</a>); public domain.</p><p>English: Morris Jastrow and Albert T. Clay, An Old Babylonian Version of the Gilgamesh Epic (Yale University Press, 1920): the Pennsylvania Tablet, col. I-II. Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/An_Old_Babylonian_Version_of_the_Gilgamesh_Epic%2FThe_Pennsylvania_Tablet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">An Old Babylonian Version of the Gilgamesh Epic/The Pennsylvania Tablet</a>); public domain.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (2)</summary><ul><li>The Akkadian is given as Jastrow and Clay transliterated it in 1920, sign by sign; square brackets mark words restored where the tablet is broken.</li><li>Their reading of the hero's name was &quot;Gish&quot; (Gilgamesh); Erech is the biblical name of Uruk.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
